window.__LIVE_NEWS__["2026-09-10"] = {"date":"2026-09-10","articles":[{"id":"n1789017820_0","date":"2026-09-10","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Iran war won't end until after crucial November elections, says Trump","summary":"The president also said oil prices won't come down until after the November races, claiming without evidence that Iran wants to impact the election.","body":"BBC News Iran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump To play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nWatch: Trump says Iran war will end immediately after US midterm elections\n\nUS President Donald Trump has said he does not think the Iran war will end until after November&#x27;s midterm elections, adding that oil prices will not come down until then.\n\nI think the war&#x27;s going to end immediately after the election because they can&#x27;t hold out any longer, Trump said of Tehran on Wednesday.\n\nHe made the comments as he headed to Texas for the party&#x27;s first-ever midterm election convention, when the balance of power could change in the US government.\n\nTrump is facing falling polling numbers and pressure from within his Republican Party to end the war with Iran. On Wednesday, oil prices rose to $100 (£74) for the first time since July due to increased fighting in the Middle East.\n\nRight after the election, oil prices are going to be tumbling downward, Trump told reporters. I think it&#x27;s going to take a little bit longer than the midterm.\n\nHe added that Iran was desperate to try and affect the election, so that we can get a nice weak group of people in there - an apparent reference to the Democrats - and leave them alone and let them have their nuclear weapon .\n\nHe provided no proof of the election claim. Tehran has repeatedly denied it is seeking nuclear weapons.\n\nAt the convention in Texas, Trump echoed some of these lines, saying the price of oil would tumble as soon as we win the war with Iran .\n\nIn his speech, he also referenced dissatisfaction among some Americans on the issue: Guys come up to me and say, &#x27;I wish you didn&#x27;t do the war in Iran. Now gasoline is up&#x27; .\n\nWatch: Do Republicans blame Trump for high oil prices?\n\nPublished 3 hours ago Trump says Iran war will end &#x27;immediately after&#x27; midterms as he heads to Republican convention\n\nTrump also said further negotiations with Iran could possibly happen, but it&#x27;s not something we&#x27;re looking at .\n\nThe US president initially predicted the war, which is now in its seventh month, would last only a matter of weeks. The conflict, and its knock-on effects on everything from fuel to groceries, has led to a steep decline in his approval ratings - and pressure from those within his party, including devout supporters, to quickly end the war.\n\nMidterm elections will be held on 3 November, and Republicans are in danger of losing their control on Congress.\n\nAt the convention, Trump sought to energise the party ahead of November. Historically, a sitting president&#x27;s party tends to lose seats in Congress in the midterm elections.\n\nFighting in the Middle East has intensified in recent days, as oil facilities throughout the region come under attack, driving up the global price of fuel and any item that requires shipping.\n\nThe price of oil hit $100 a barrel on Wednesday for the first time since July. Similarly, earlier this week the price of US diesel hit an all-time-high , which is likely to cause a ripple effect on prices across the US.\n\nOn Wednesday, the US military said it had attacked five oil tanker ships with ties to Iran. Tehran, in response, launched attacks on two US Navy destroyers and eight oil tankers in the Strait of Hormuz, causing heavy damage to the vessels, according to Iranian state media.\n\nA day earlier, the Iran-linked Houthis in Yemen launched a wave of attacks in Saudi Arabia, sparking fires at oil facilities.\n\nThe fighting, which began again recently after a 60-day ceasefire between the US and Iran formally expired last month, has also raised predictions that the rising fuel costs could lead to higher interest rates in the US.\n\nOil hits $100 a barrel for first time since July after US and Houthi strikes\n\nUS and Iran trade retaliatory attacks on ships as conflict flares\n\nHow could US-Iran conflict end? Three experts give their views\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:43 Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 00:00:43, play video Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 1:15 Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk. 00:01:15, play video Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk 1:00 Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 00:01:00, play video Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 0:36 Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests. 00:00:36, play video Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests 1:14 Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled. 00:01:14, play video Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled 1:11 RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC. 00:01:11, play video RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC 1:04 Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life. 00:01:04, play video Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life 0:56 How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements. 00:00:56, play video How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements 0:30 &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 00:00:30, play video &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 0:34 Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl. 00:00:34, play video Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl 0:27 The curse of living with an early bird. 00:00:27, play video The curse of living with an early bird 1:17 &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 00:01:17, play video &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 1:07 Why do people get ill at Freshers? 00:01:07, play video Why do people get ill at Freshers? 0:59 Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase. 00:00:59, play video Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase 0:37 UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank. 00:00:37, play video UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank 1:54 Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow. 00:01:54, play video Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow 1:01 Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms. 00:01:01, play video Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms 2:28 Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone. 00:02:28, play video Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone Top stories England&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;It&#x27;s been a nightmare&#x27;: Passengers face airport chaos after air traffic issue\n\nDallas Cowboys Cheerleaders star impresses on Broadway after celebrity casting row\n\nDivorcing abusive husband cost woman £105k\n\nEight-year-old boy runs 32 miles and scales 15 summits in a single day\n\nI&#x27;m 27 and I&#x27;ve already written my will - here&#x27;s why\n\n&#x27;Constantly on my mind&#x27; - 9/11 agony endures for bereaved, 25 years on\n\nVery hot drinks may damage the food pipe and increase cancer risk\n\n&#x27;Emotional rollercoaster&#x27; - Lindsay Clancy jurors speak out on what led to mistrial\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nThe warm-hearted Aussie rom-com has returned\n\nLily Allen on how her vocal talent was spotted in school\n\nWhy Dolly Parton meant so much to so many around the world\n\nChew over how the Norman Conquest changed our diet\n\nLady Joan&#x27;s death linked to lack of medication\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;Farage faces Met inquiry&#x27; and &#x27;Ready for departure?&#x27;\n\n&#x27;My cancer spread while I waited months for surgery&#x27;: The cost of England&#x27;s cancer wait times\n\nEngland&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nAir traffic failure was avoidable, says transport secretary\n\nWe spent £23,000 on fertility treatment - now our embryos will be destroyed\n\nAI is becoming harder to control – can humans stay in charge?\n\nThe one thing you need to do to succeed - according to top bosses\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5ydld0gykpo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"negotiation","phonetic":"/nɪˌɡəʊʃiˈeɪʃn/","definition":"谈判","example":"Published 3 hours ago Trump says Iran war will end &#x27;immediately after&#x27; midterms as he heads to Republican convention\n\nTrump also said further negotiations with Iran could possibly happen, but it&#x27;s not some","_bank_example":"Negotiations resume on Tuesday.","example2":"Negotiations resume on Tuesday."},{"word":"predictions","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 预测；预言 (predict 预测)","example":"The fighting, which began again recently after a 60-day ceasefire between the US and Iran formally expired last month, has also raised predictions that the rising fuel costs could lead to higher interest rates in the US.","example2":"The report highlighted predictions as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"plural of predic-tion"},{"word":"responsible","phonetic":"","definition":"负责的","example":"The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites.","example2":"The report describes the responsible impact on local communities.","en_def":"a. worthy of or requiring responsibility or trust; or held accountable；s. being the agent or cause"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"被动语态 (Passive Voice)","explanation":"be 动词 + 过去分词。新闻大量使用被动语态，因为动作的承受者比执行者更重要。注意 by 短语引出真正的执行者，常被省略。","en":"The passive voice emphasizes the action or its receiver rather than the doer: be + past participle. Used heavily in news when the doer is unknown or less important. The agent can be added with 'by' or omitted entirely.","example":"This video can not be played\n\nWatch: Trump says Iran war will end immediately after US midterm elections\n\nUS President Donald Trump has said he does not think the Iran war will end until after November&#x27;s midterm ele","example2":"The bridge was built in 1932 and is still used by thousands of commuters every day."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"lead to","meaning":"导致（lead to sanctions）","en":"\"lead to\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"lead to sanctions\".","example":"The fighting, which began again recently after a 60-day ceasefire between the US and Iran formally expired last month, has also raised predictions that the rising fuel costs could lead to higher interest rates in the US.","example2":"The shortage led to panic buying in several cities."}]},{"id":"n1789017820_1","date":"2026-09-10","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Four takeaways from Trump's speech at first Republican midterms convention","summary":"He covered many of the subjects he has returned to time and again - with a few surprises thrown in.","body":"BBC News Four takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention To play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nWatch: Trump tells supporters their vote will decide if country stumbles or moves forward\n\nThe Republican Party&#x27;s first midterms convention had all the trappings of the traditional presidential election-year gathering: supporters in sparkling costumes, a conveyor belt of candidate speeches, and vendors selling campaign merchandise.\n\nBut unlike an ordinary convention, no official party business was conducted in Dallas, with the event instead serving as a rally for Republicans who are seeking to boost turnout and avoid losing control of Congress in November.\n\nPresident Donald Trump, whose grip over the party remains absolute, delivered a lengthy keynote speech on Wednesday during the opening night of the two-day event. He covered many of the subjects he has returned to time and again this year - with a few surprises thrown in.\n\nHere are the key takeaways from his address.\n\nThe vast arena was filled with diehard Trump supporters sporting red MAGA gear from his past presidential campaigns. A decade after his first White House run, Trump is still beloved among his base and he has bent the party to his will in the years since.\n\nThe old-style Republican establishment, or what&#x27;s left of it in the Trump era, was nowhere to be seen. Senator Susan Collins of Maine and other Republicans running in competitive races chose to stay away.\n\nImage source, Getty Images The absence of more moderate Republicans was glaring and offered a reminder of just how much the party has changed.\n\nIt also raised questions about the effectiveness of holding an unprecedented midterm convention in the first place.\n\nThe core audience, both in the arena and watching on television, appeared to be Trump loyalists, not the moderates and swing voters the Republican Party needs to turn out in large numbers in November.\n\nTrump&#x27;s total grip on the party raises some pointed questions about its future. Namely - who will replace him? And more immediately for November&#x27;s elections, how will it perform when his name isn&#x27;t on the ballot?\n\nImage source, Getty Images Trump offered a simple solution to the latter question on Wednesday night. After warning that the sitting president tends to lose the midterms, he told the crowd: I&#x27;m asking you to pretend that I&#x27;m on the ballot.\n\nThe remark was an acknowledgement by Trump that he remains the party&#x27;s best driver of turnout. It also speaks to the challenge Republicans have both in November and in the next presidential election in 2028.\n\nTrump will not be on the ballot again, and though he can continue campaigning for the party, there is not a clear or obvious successor who can rally the base quite like Trump.\n\nIn interviews with the BBC, convention goers often looked ahead to the election in two years&#x27; time and debated who Trump&#x27;s successor should be. Vice-President JD Vance and Secretary of State Marco Rubio were frequently mentioned as top contenders.\n\nTrump, however, made clear in Dallas that he is still in charge and is not in a hurry to give up the spotlight.\n\nEarlier on Wednesday, as he departed for the convention, Trump acknowledged that Americans may have to wait a while longer to see relief from high fuel prices.\n\nI think it&#x27;s going to take a little bit longer than the midterms, Trump said.\n\nThe president reiterated that message in his speech, arguing that short-term economic pain caused by the war in Iran was worth it to prevent Tehran from developing nuclear weapons.\n\nBut that may be a difficult message to land with voters who are feeling higher prices.\n\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nWatch: Do Republicans blame Trump for high oil prices?\n\nPolls have consistently indicated in recent months that the cost of living is a top-ranking issue among Americans.\n\nTrump&#x27;s approval rating, too, has taken a hit as the war in Iran has dragged on. A Financial Times / Focaldata poll released on Sunday indicated that it had hit a new low, with only 33% of registered voters approving of his presidential performance.\n\nMany Republican candidates may well have hoped for a more direct message on addressing the cost of living and quickly ending the war in Iran.\n\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nWatch: Trump says US adults will get $5,000 if Republicans win midterms\n\nTrump has long thrown out unconventional ideas at rallies. On Wednesday night, that came in the form of an offer to pay every adult US citizen $5,000 (£3,700) if Republicans win both chambers of Congress in November.\n\nThe only caveat, Trump said, was the money would have to be spent in the US.\n\nHe offered no details on how the plan would work, where the money would come from, or how the US government would monitor how it was spent. Democrats quickly labelled it an empty promise and others questioned whether such a pledge was even legal.\n\nThere are nearly 270 million American adults, which means a $5,000 payout would cost the US government some $1.3 trillion.\n\nWhether the plan was possible or not appeared to be beside the point. The idea drew raucous applause at the convention and Trump seemed to relish the moment.\n\nWatch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:43 Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 00:00:43, play video Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 1:15 Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk. 00:01:15, play video Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk 1:00 Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 00:01:00, play video Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 0:36 Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests. 00:00:36, play video Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests 1:14 Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled. 00:01:14, play video Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled 1:11 RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC. 00:01:11, play video RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC 1:04 Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life. 00:01:04, play video Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life 0:56 How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements. 00:00:56, play video How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements 0:30 &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 00:00:30, play video &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 0:34 Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl. 00:00:34, play video Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl 0:27 The curse of living with an early bird. 00:00:27, play video The curse of living with an early bird 1:17 &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 00:01:17, play video &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 1:07 Why do people get ill at Freshers? 00:01:07, play video Why do people get ill at Freshers? 0:59 Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase. 00:00:59, play video Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase 0:37 UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank. 00:00:37, play video UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank 1:54 Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow. 00:01:54, play video Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow 1:01 Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms. 00:01:01, play video Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms 2:28 Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone. 00:02:28, play video Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone Top stories England&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;It&#x27;s been a nightmare&#x27;: Passengers face airport chaos after air traffic issue\n\nDallas Cowboys Cheerleaders star impresses on Broadway after celebrity casting row\n\nDivorcing abusive husband cost woman £105k\n\nEight-year-old boy runs 32 miles and scales 15 summits in a single day\n\nI&#x27;m 27 and I&#x27;ve already written my will - here&#x27;s why\n\n&#x27;Constantly on my mind&#x27; - 9/11 agony endures for bereaved, 25 years on\n\nVery hot drinks may damage the food pipe and increase cancer risk\n\n&#x27;Emotional rollercoaster&#x27; - Lindsay Clancy jurors speak out on what led to mistrial\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nThe warm-hearted Aussie rom-com has returned\n\nLily Allen on how her vocal talent was spotted in school\n\nWhy Dolly Parton meant so much to so many around the world\n\nChew over how the Norman Conquest changed our diet\n\nLady Joan&#x27;s death linked to lack of medication\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;Farage faces Met inquiry&#x27; and &#x27;Ready for departure?&#x27;\n\n&#x27;My cancer spread while I waited months for surgery&#x27;: The cost of England&#x27;s cancer wait times\n\nEngland&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nAir traffic failure was avoidable, says transport secretary\n\nWe spent £23,000 on fertility treatment - now our embryos will be destroyed\n\nAI is becoming harder to control – can humans stay in charge?\n\nThe one thing you need to do to succeed - according to top bosses\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cg7kjlzlev1o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"establishment","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 确立, 制定, 设施\\ / [经] 企业, 公司, 商店","example":"The old-style Republican establishment, or what&#x27;s left of it in the Trump era, was nowhere to be seen.","example2":"The report highlighted establishment as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"a public or private structure (business or governmental or educational) including buildings and equipment for business or residence；any large organization"},{"word":"effectiveness","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 效力\\ /  有效性, 能行性","example":"It also raised questions about the effectiveness of holding an unprecedented midterm convention in the first place.","example2":"The report describes the effectiveness impact on local communities.","en_def":"power to be effective; the quality of being able to bring about an effect"},{"word":"traditional","phonetic":"","definition":"传统的","example":"This video can not be played\n\nWatch: Trump tells supporters their vote will decide if country stumbles or moves forward\n\nThe Republican Party&#x27;s first midterms convention had all the trappings of the traditional pres","example2":"Many voters expressed traditional support for the new measure.","en_def":"a. consisting of or derived from tradition；s. pertaining to time-honored orthodox doctrines"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"间接引语 / 转述动词 (Reported Speech)","explanation":"said/told/warned/confirmed + that 从句（that 常省略）。新闻标题后正文常用转述动词引述当事人观点，注意从句语序不倒装。","en":"Reported (indirect) speech reports what someone said without using their exact words, typically with a reporting verb (said, told, added) and a tense shift: 'She said she was leaving.'","example":"After warning that the sitting president tends to lose the midterms, he told the crowd: I&#x27;m asking you to pretend that I&#x27;m on the ballot.","example2":"She told reporters (that) the deal had already been signed."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"take a toll","meaning":"造成损害（take a heavy toll）","en":"\"take a toll\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"take a heavy toll\".","example":"","example2":"Years of war have taken a heavy toll on civilians."}]},{"id":"n1789017820_2","date":"2026-09-10","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Five dead and 86 missing as 'heartbreaking' fire breaks out on Philippines ferry","summary":"The fire broke out as the vessel nearing its destination at the tourist hotspot of Coron in Palawan.","body":"BBC News Five dead and 86 missing as &#x27;heartbreaking&#x27; fire breaks out on Philippines ferry Image source, Philippine Coast Guard Image caption, The fire broke out as the vessel nearing its destination at the tourist hotspot of Coron in Palawan\n\nAt least five people have died and 86 are missing after a fire broke out on a ferry in the Philippines, the country&#x27;s coastguard said.\n\nThe MV June Aster, which set off from the capital Manila ferrying 117 passengers and 17 crew members, was nearing its destination at the tourist hotspot of Coron in the province of Palawan when it caught fire.\n\nThe blaze, which erupted at 18:00 local time (10:00 GMT) Wednesday, was still burning as of 11:00 on Thursday. The strong currents and poor weather were a challenge to those working to extinguish it, the coastguard said.\n\nA first responder told the BBC they had never seen such a disaster in Coron, calling it really heartbreaking .\n\nI was one of the people who carried and transported the casualties to the hospital last night, the first responder said, adding that the ferry was an hour away from its destination.\n\nVideo clips shared by the coastguard on Wednesday night showed the vessel engulfed in flames and people being pulled out of the water into rescue boats.\n\nReports from our vessel indicate strong currents in the area, a spokesman from the coastguard tell the BBC, noting that the ferry had drifted from its initial distress location – four nautical miles off Barangay Turda – to a point 7.68 nautical miles away.\n\nIt has been carried further out by the current. These are the challenges our personnel are facing in the search area.\n\nThe cause of the fire remains under investigation, the coastguard said, adding that it had not yet spoken to any of the 43 people who&#x27;d been rescued.\n\nThe survivors were taken to an evacuation centre in Barangay Turda, which sits just north of Marcilla. The state of their injuries is unclear.\n\nAnelyn Magbanua is among those anxiously waiting for news of their loved ones.\n\nHer husband Marlon had spent a few days in Manila visiting their two children, who were going to school in the capital city.\n\nMagbanua last heard from him while the ferry was passing through a village about two hours from the port in Coron, she tells the BBC.\n\nI was expecting that the ferry will arrive at the pier at between 18:00 and 19:00 yesterday, she said, adding that she had gone to bed early but was awakened by a cousin who told her the ferry Marlon was on had caught fire.\n\nI couldn&#x27;t describe what I am feeling right now. I have been crying and praying since last night, Magbanua says.\n\nShe&#x27;d travelled to a hospital in Coron, hoping to find Marlon among the survivors – but he remains unaccounted for.\n\nMy two-year-old son and I are outside the hospital as we speak. We have nothing but ourselves, she says.\n\nImage source, Reuters Image caption, Footage from the coastguard show the vessel engulfed in flames\n\nRai Garcia has three family friends among those missing.\n\nMae Lorraine Galera-Navoa, who is the sister of Garcia&#x27;s brother-in-law, had travelled to Luzon to attend her father&#x27;s funeral, accompanied by her husband Elyzer Navoa and their three-year-old daughter Hiraya Joy.\n\nThey had been travelling home to El Nido in Palawan on board the vessel when it caught fire.\n\nWe haven&#x27;t received any news yet. That&#x27;s why we had to comment on local news threads just to receive updates, Garcia says.\n\nWe still hope and pray for their safety.\n\nGarcia and his family made a poster to appeal for information of the family of three using a photo Mae had taken on the ferry and shared with immediate relatives.\n\nOthers have posted on the Philippine Coast Guard&#x27;s Facebook page asking for information of their missing loved ones. Please can you find my sibling, read one comment.\n\nMaritime accidents are common in the Philippines, an archipelago of 7,100 islands.\n\nInter-island ferries are a cheap way to move people and cargo between islands, some of which are inaccessible by air.\n\nBut there is always the risk of severe weather, monsoon rains and typhoons which can cause turbulent conditions. The country is affected by an average of 20 typhoons every year.\n\nPast accidents have also been blamed on poor maintenance and uneven enforcement of safety laws.\n\nIn 2023, 31 people died after a fire broke out at a passenger ferry in the country&#x27;s south-western tip. The MV Lady Mary Joy 3 was on its way to Basilan island from Zamboanga city, a major port in the main southern island of Mindanao.\n\nBut with 86 still missing, this ferry disaster could prove to be among the worst to have happened in the Philippines in recent years.\n\nWatch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:43 Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 00:00:43, play video Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 1:15 Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk. 00:01:15, play video Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk 1:00 Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 00:01:00, play video Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 0:36 Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests. 00:00:36, play video Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests 1:14 Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled. 00:01:14, play video Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled 1:11 RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC. 00:01:11, play video RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC 1:04 Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life. 00:01:04, play video Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life 0:56 How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements. 00:00:56, play video How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements 0:30 &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 00:00:30, play video &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 0:34 Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl. 00:00:34, play video Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl 0:27 The curse of living with an early bird. 00:00:27, play video The curse of living with an early bird 1:17 &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 00:01:17, play video &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 1:07 Why do people get ill at Freshers? 00:01:07, play video Why do people get ill at Freshers? 0:59 Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase. 00:00:59, play video Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase 0:37 UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank. 00:00:37, play video UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank 1:54 Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow. 00:01:54, play video Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow 1:01 Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms. 00:01:01, play video Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms 2:28 Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone. 00:02:28, play video Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone Top stories England&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;It&#x27;s been a nightmare&#x27;: Passengers face airport chaos after air traffic issue\n\nDallas Cowboys Cheerleaders star impresses on Broadway after celebrity casting row\n\nDivorcing abusive husband cost woman £105k\n\nEight-year-old boy runs 32 miles and scales 15 summits in a single day\n\nI&#x27;m 27 and I&#x27;ve already written my will - here&#x27;s why\n\n&#x27;Constantly on my mind&#x27; - 9/11 agony endures for bereaved, 25 years on\n\nVery hot drinks may damage the food pipe and increase cancer risk\n\n&#x27;Emotional rollercoaster&#x27; - Lindsay Clancy jurors speak out on what led to mistrial\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nThe warm-hearted Aussie rom-com has returned\n\nLily Allen on how her vocal talent was spotted in school\n\nWhy Dolly Parton meant so much to so many around the world\n\nChew over how the Norman Conquest changed our diet\n\nLady Joan&#x27;s death linked to lack of medication\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;Farage faces Met inquiry&#x27; and &#x27;Ready for departure?&#x27;\n\n&#x27;My cancer spread while I waited months for surgery&#x27;: The cost of England&#x27;s cancer wait times\n\nEngland&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nAir traffic failure was avoidable, says transport secretary\n\nWe spent £23,000 on fertility treatment - now our embryos will be destroyed\n\nAI is becoming harder to control – can humans stay in charge?\n\nThe one thing you need to do to succeed - according to top bosses\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cy4zd4dn9n9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"investigation","phonetic":"/ɪnˌvestɪˈɡeɪʃn/","definition":"调查","example":"The cause of the fire remains under investigation, the coastguard said, adding that it had not yet spoken to any of the 43 people who&#x27;d been rescued.","_bank_example":"A BBC investigation found the accounts.","example2":"A full investigation into the crash is under way."},{"word":"destination","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 目的地, 目标, 目的\\ / [计] 目的文件, 目的单元","example":"BBC News Five dead and 86 missing as &#x27;heartbreaking&#x27; fire breaks out on Philippines ferry Image source, Philippine Coast Guard Image caption, The fire broke out as the vessel nearing its destination at the tour","example2":"The latest figures show a clear link between the economy and destination.","en_def":"the ultimate goal for which something is done"},{"word":"enforcement","phonetic":"","definition":"执法；执行","example":"Past accidents have also been blamed on poor maintenance and uneven enforcement of safety laws.","example2":"A growing number of people now call attention to enforcement.","en_def":"the act of enforcing; ensuring observance of or obedience to"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"定语从句 (Relative Clause)","explanation":"who/which/that 引导的定语从句修饰前面的名词。who 指人、which 指物，that 两者皆可；关系代词在从句中作宾语时可省略。","en":"A relative clause modifies a noun using who (people), which (things), or that (both). When the relative pronoun is the object it can be omitted: 'the law (that) the Senate passed'.","example":"The MV June Aster, which set off from the capital Manila ferrying 117 passengers and 17 crew members, was nearing its destination at the tourist hotspot of Coron in the province of Palawan when it caught fire.","example2":"The scientist who developed the vaccine won the Nobel Prize."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"behind bars","meaning":"在狱中","en":"in prison; locked up e.g. \"He spent five years behind bars for fraud.\"","example":"","example2":"He spent five years behind bars for fraud."}]},{"id":"n1789017820_3","date":"2026-09-10","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Spanish intelligence warned of Ceuta mass crossing plans before surge","summary":"Declassified documents show Spanish and Moroccan authorities were alerted to social media calls for a mass migrant crossing the day before tens of thousands entered Ceuta.","body":"BBC News Spanish intelligence warned of Ceuta mass crossing plans before surge Image source, Reuters By Guy Hedgecoe Reporting from Madrid Published 7 hours ago Spanish intelligence services warned of social media calls for a mass migrant crossing into Ceuta the day before tens of thousands of people entered the territory, newly released government documents show.\n\nThe revelations emerged in documents declassified by the Spanish government amid accusations it failed to give the full story of the migrant influx.\n\nOn 29 July, Spain&#x27;s National Intelligence Service (CNI) told Moroccan and Ceuta authorities that social media groups were encouraging migrants to cross the following day.\n\nPrime Minister Pedro Sánchez has insisted that the events of 30 July were impossible to predict, but opposition parties say the documents show the government misled Spaniards.\n\nSánchez had previously told parliament that there was no information that conveyed the magnitude of the crisis we were going to suffer on the last two days of July.\n\nAt least 72,000 migrants crossed from Morocco into Ceuta on 30 and 31 July. Most swam around a border fence and at least 100 people died during the crossing, according to figures from Ceuta&#x27;s mayor.\n\nAlthough most returned, as many as 5,000 remain in makeshift camps or are sleeping on the city&#x27;s streets and beaches, leading to unrest in the autonomous Spanish city in North Africa. Locals have expressed safety concerns and some have destroyed shelters and blocked aid being supplied to the migrants .\n\nIn a note on 29 July, the CNI told Moroccan intelligence it had identified WhatsApp and Facebook groups promoting an attempt by migrants to reach the Spanish exclave the following day, both swimming and via the [border] fence .\n\nThe CNI reported that around 180,000 people were receiving the messages, one of which read: Whoever wants to cross has the last chance on Throne Day [30 July] at 8 the authorities will be distracted with the celebrations and parades.\n\nIt also warned, via text message, the central government&#x27;s representation in Ceuta and the civil guard about the social media activity.\n\nThe messages are part of a tranche of more than 40 security documents linked to the Ceuta crossing that the government declassified following mounting pressure on Sánchez over his handling of the crisis and widespread allegations in Spain that Morocco had played a role in facilitating the crossings.\n\nEarlier this month, a leaked Spanish police report seemed to implicate Moroccan police in the incident.\n\nHowever, Sánchez has maintained that there is no evidence suggesting Moroccan complicity.\n\nShortly before the documents were released on Wednesday, he told Spanish television that the mass arrival of migrants was not expected .\n\nBut opposition politicians said the newly released material contradict that claim.\n\nThey had the information, they are directly responsible for the invasion of Ceuta and for those who died, said Ester Muñoz of the conservative People&#x27;s Party.\n\nJorge Buxadé, of the far-right Vox, said on social media that the documents showed that Sánchez and his band of criminals should be brought before the Supreme Court for treason and security crimes .\n\nThe government has continued to insist that it acted according to the information available.\n\nForeign Minister José Manuel Albares said he was not aware of any report that might have forseen the magnitude of what happened on July 30 .\n\nA government source said the published documents showed that the information available before the mass crossing did not make it possible to anticipate the scale that the crisis would reach or prepare with enough time the necessary response .\n\nMiguel Ángel Pérez Triano, the central government&#x27;s delegate in Ceuta, addressed the revelations about the text messages the CNI had sent to his office the day before the mass crossing.\n\nThe Whatsapps from [July] 29 do not constitute an alert or a report, he said. It is nothing more than an exchange of information.\n\nProtests in Spain against government&#x27;s handling of Ceuta migrant crisis\n\nSexual assaults happening almost every day in Ceuta, prosecutors say\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:43 Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 00:00:43, play video Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 1:15 Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk. 00:01:15, play video Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk 1:00 Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 00:01:00, play video Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 0:36 Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests. 00:00:36, play video Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests 1:14 Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled. 00:01:14, play video Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled 1:11 RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC. 00:01:11, play video RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC 1:04 Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life. 00:01:04, play video Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life 0:56 How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements. 00:00:56, play video How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements 0:30 &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 00:00:30, play video &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 0:34 Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl. 00:00:34, play video Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl 0:27 The curse of living with an early bird. 00:00:27, play video The curse of living with an early bird 1:17 &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 00:01:17, play video &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 1:07 Why do people get ill at Freshers? 00:01:07, play video Why do people get ill at Freshers? 0:59 Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase. 00:00:59, play video Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase 0:37 UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank. 00:00:37, play video UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank 1:54 Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow. 00:01:54, play video Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow 1:01 Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms. 00:01:01, play video Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms 2:28 Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone. 00:02:28, play video Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone Top stories England&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;It&#x27;s been a nightmare&#x27;: Passengers face airport chaos after air traffic issue\n\nDallas Cowboys Cheerleaders star impresses on Broadway after celebrity casting row\n\nDivorcing abusive husband cost woman £105k\n\nEight-year-old boy runs 32 miles and scales 15 summits in a single day\n\nI&#x27;m 27 and I&#x27;ve already written my will - here&#x27;s why\n\n&#x27;Constantly on my mind&#x27; - 9/11 agony endures for bereaved, 25 years on\n\nVery hot drinks may damage the food pipe and increase cancer risk\n\n&#x27;Emotional rollercoaster&#x27; - Lindsay Clancy jurors speak out on what led to mistrial\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nThe warm-hearted Aussie rom-com has returned\n\nLily Allen on how her vocal talent was spotted in school\n\nWhy Dolly Parton meant so much to so many around the world\n\nChew over how the Norman Conquest changed our diet\n\nLady Joan&#x27;s death linked to lack of medication\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;Farage faces Met inquiry&#x27; and &#x27;Ready for departure?&#x27;\n\nEngland&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nAir traffic failure was avoidable, says transport secretary\n\nWe spent £23,000 on fertility treatment - now our embryos will be destroyed\n\nThe one thing you need to do to succeed - according to top bosses\n\nAI is becoming harder to control – can humans stay in charge?\n\nPrince William, Kane and Shearer in campaign to use sport to prevent suicide\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ced7vj2gv82o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"intelligence","phonetic":"","definition":"情报；智力","example":"Spanish intelligence warned of Ceuta mass crossing plans before surge.","example2":"The latest figures show a clear link between the economy and intelligence.","en_def":"the ability to comprehend; to understand and profit from experience；a unit responsible for gathering and interpreting information about an enemy"},{"word":"conservative","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 保守的, 守旧的, 有保存力的\\ / [化] 防腐剂; 保存剂","example":"They had the information, they are directly responsible for the invasion of Ceuta and for those who died, said Ester Muñoz of the conservative People&#x27;s Party.","example2":"The report highlighted conservative as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"a person who is reluctant to accept changes and new ideas；a member of a Conservative Party"},{"word":"authorities","phonetic":"/ɔːˈθɒrətiz/","definition":"当局","example":"On 29 July, Spain&#x27;s National Intelligence Service (CNI) told Moroccan and Ceuta authorities that social media groups were encouraging migrants to cross the following day.","_bank_example":"Authorities urged calm.","example2":"The authorities have launched a full investigation into the incident.","en_def":"the people or organizations that have official power and responsibility"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"现在完成时 (Present Perfect)","explanation":"have/has + 过去分词。表示过去发生、对现在有影响的动作。新闻中常用来强调\"已经发生\"的结果，常与 already、just、so far 连用。","en":"The present perfect (have/has + past participle) links a past action to the present moment. Used when the exact time is not given or the result still matters now: 'Prices have fallen sharply.'","example":"Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez has insisted that the events of 30 July were impossible to predict, but opposition parties say the documents show the government misled Spaniards.","example2":"Prices have fallen sharply since January, and analysts expect the trend to continue."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"grapple with","meaning":"费力应对（grapple with inflation）","en":"to struggle hard to deal with a difficult problem e.g. \"Policymakers are still grappling with rising inflation.\"","example":"","example2":"Policymakers are still grappling with rising inflation."}]},{"id":"n1789017820_4","date":"2026-09-10","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Google picks Finland for its largest single investment in Europe","summary":"The US tech giant says the €13bn data centre project will create tens of thousands of jobs.","body":"BBC News Google picks Finland for its largest single investment in Europe Image source, Getty Images By Jessica Rawnsley Published 9 September 2026 Google will buy up to half the electricity produced by one of Finland&#x27;s nuclear power plants as part of a record €13bn (£11bn; $15bn) investment in the country&#x27;s AI infrastructure, the company announced.\n\nThe US tech giant said the package would fund three new data centres, expand an existing site and support energy projects to power growing demand for AI services.\n\nThis is Google&#x27;s largest single investment in Europe and a testament to Finland&#x27;s leadership in responsibly building AI infrastructure, the company said on Wednesday.\n\nGoogle&#x27;s decision is a clear testament to our strengths, Finland&#x27;s Prime Minister Petteri Orpo said in a statement.\n\nAs part of the deal, Google signed a 22-year contract with Finnish utility Fortum to buy up to 50% of the output from the Loviisa nuclear power plant.\n\nEarlier this week, TikTok announced a $1bn investment in Finland to build a new data centre in Kouvola, pointing to the country&#x27;s strong digital infrastructure, clean energy mix, robust data governance and skilled tech talent .\n\nFinland has emerged as an attractive location for data centres because of its cool climate, plentiful low-carbon electricity and relatively uncongested power grid. The colder temperatures can reduce the energy needed to cool facilities packed with computer servers.\n\nTechnology companies are racing to secure power supplies as the AI boom drives demand for vast networks of data centres.\n\nThe Google investment is expected to support more than 37,000 jobs during construction and boost Finland&#x27;s GDP by €3.6bn a year, according to Google. Construction is set to take place in 2027 and 2028.\n\nThe value of the data economy extends far beyond direct investment into spurring innovation, research and development, Orpo added.\n\nDeepening our collaboration with Google will deliver lasting benefits for both parties.\n\nGoogle will build new data centres in Kajaani, Muhos and Vaala, while expanding its existing facility in Hamina, which it established after converting a former paper mill in 2009.\n\nThe infrastructure will help support products such as its AI chatbot Gemini, alongside services including Search, Maps and YouTube.\n\nGoogle said the investments also covered clean energy projects, and dedicated nature and community funds to support local biodiversity, education, research, and workforce development .\n\nGoogle is proud to deepen our roots in Finland, said Ruth Porat, president and chief investment officer of Alphabet and Google.\n\nThis investment underscores Google&#x27;s commitment to grow our presence responsibly, pairing the expansion of our technical infrastructure with new energy capacity, grid enhancements, and energy affordability initiatives, she added.\n\nEarlier this year, Google&#x27;s parent company Alphabet raised its global spending plans to as much as $205bn as it sought to expand computing capacity for AI services.\n\nThe agreement with Fortum provides long-term financial certainty for the Loviisa plant, which currently generates about 10% of Finland&#x27;s electricity.\n\nFortum said Google&#x27;s commitment would support an investment programme aimed at extending the life of the nuclear station and increasing its generating capacity.\n\nAI data centres are booming in Australia - but at what cost?\n\nGoogle to invest $15bn to build AI data hub in India\n\nNvidia revenue doubles on continued AI demand\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:43 Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 00:00:43, play video Why has the BBC dropped Drag Race UK? 1:15 Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk. 00:01:15, play video Drinking very hot drinks may increase cancer risk 1:00 Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 00:01:00, play video Teens missing education going &#x27;under the radar&#x27; 0:36 Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests. 00:00:36, play video Learner drivers still forced to wait months to book tests 1:14 Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled. 00:01:14, play video Flight to Italy for couple&#x27;s wedding cancelled 1:11 RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC. 00:01:11, play video RNLI volunteer targeted online speaks to the BBC 1:04 Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life. 00:01:04, play video Reuniting with the teacher who changed my life 0:56 How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements. 00:00:56, play video How Israel responded to UK sanctions on West Bank settlements 0:30 &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 00:00:30, play video &#x27;There&#x27;s isn&#x27;t really a cheat code to grief&#x27; 0:34 Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl. 00:00:34, play video Midweek meal - crispy mango tofu quinoa bowl 0:27 The curse of living with an early bird. 00:00:27, play video The curse of living with an early bird 1:17 &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 00:01:17, play video &#x27;A E failures ruined my son&#x27;s life&#x27; 1:07 Why do people get ill at Freshers? 00:01:07, play video Why do people get ill at Freshers? 0:59 Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase. 00:00:59, play video Highest paid world leader to get $1m salary increase 0:37 UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank. 00:00:37, play video UK to ban imports from Israeli settlements in West Bank 1:54 Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow. 00:01:54, play video Palestinian parents fear for children at school as settler attacks grow 1:01 Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms. 00:01:01, play video Australians to be able to switch off social media algorithms 2:28 Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone. 00:02:28, play video Residents fear drivers racing could kill someone Top stories England&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;It&#x27;s been a nightmare&#x27;: Passengers face airport chaos after air traffic issue\n\nDallas Cowboys Cheerleaders star impresses on Broadway after celebrity casting row\n\nDivorcing abusive husband cost woman £105k\n\nEight-year-old boy runs 32 miles and scales 15 summits in a single day\n\nI&#x27;m 27 and I&#x27;ve already written my will - here&#x27;s why\n\n&#x27;Constantly on my mind&#x27; - 9/11 agony endures for bereaved, 25 years on\n\nVery hot drinks may damage the food pipe and increase cancer risk\n\n&#x27;Emotional rollercoaster&#x27; - Lindsay Clancy jurors speak out on what led to mistrial\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nThe warm-hearted Aussie rom-com has returned\n\nLily Allen on how her vocal talent was spotted in school\n\nWhy Dolly Parton meant so much to so many around the world\n\nChew over how the Norman Conquest changed our diet\n\nLady Joan&#x27;s death linked to lack of medication\n\nIran war won&#x27;t end until after crucial November elections, says Trump\n\nFour takeaways from Trump&#x27;s speech at first Republican midterms convention\n\n&#x27;Farage faces Met inquiry&#x27; and &#x27;Ready for departure?&#x27;\n\n&#x27;My cancer spread while I waited months for surgery&#x27;: The cost of England&#x27;s cancer wait times\n\nEngland&#x27;s mayors to be given power to introduce tourist tax\n\nAir traffic failure was avoidable, says transport secretary\n\nWe spent £23,000 on fertility treatment - now our embryos will be destroyed\n\nAI is becoming harder to control – can humans stay in charge?\n\nThe one thing you need to do to succeed - according to top bosses\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8r6y4me2g6o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"infrastructure","phonetic":"","definition":"基础设施","example":"BBC News Google picks Finland for its largest single investment in Europe Image source, Getty Images By Jessica Rawnsley Published 9 September 2026 Google will buy up to half the electricity produced by one of Finland&#x","example2":"The heavy rains badly damaged the region's infrastructure.","en_def":"the basic structure or features of a system or organization；the stock of basic facilities and capital equipment needed for the functioning of a country or area"},{"word":"collaboration","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 合作, 勾结\\ / [法] 通敌卖国者, 奸细","example":"Deepening our collaboration with Google will deliver lasting benefits for both parties.","example2":"Campaigners welcomed the move, saying it reflects growing concern about collaboration.","en_def":"act of working jointly；act of cooperating traitorously with an enemy that is occupying your country"},{"word":"construction","phonetic":"","definition":"建设","example":"The Google investment is expected to support more than 37,000 jobs during construction and boost Finland&#x27;s GDP by €3.6bn a year, according to Google.","example2":"The report highlighted construction as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"the act of constructing something；a group of words that form a constituent of a sentence and are considered as a single unit"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"将来表达 (Future: will / going to)","explanation":"will + 动词原形表预测或承诺；be going to 表计划好的事。新闻预测走势、宣布计划时高频出现。","en":"English marks the future with 'will' (spontaneous decisions, predictions) or 'going to' (plans, intentions). News uses 'will' for official decisions: 'The president will announce the plan.'","example":"BBC News Google picks Finland for its largest single investment in Europe Image source, Getty Images By Jessica Rawnsley Published 9 September 2026 Google will buy up to half the electricity produced by one of Finland&#x","example2":"Officials said the new regulation will take effect from March."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"ratchet up","meaning":"逐步升级；步步收紧","en":"to increase something gradually, step by step e.g. \"Both sides continue to ratchet up sanctions.\"","example":"","example2":"Both sides continue to ratchet up sanctions."}]}]};
